Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 23:33:24 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] SurfaceFlinger: publish the built-in screen's power state

The HDMI daemon has no event-driven signal for "the device went to sleep with a monitor attached": nothing in the framework flips a property on sleep, and polling costs wakeups. setPowerModeInternal sees every power change on the built-in screen, so it publishes it as sys.xdplus.screen_power (0 asleep, 1 awake). The daemon sleeps on that property and switches the HDMI output off after a user-set timeout, back on at wake. The external's own state would not do: once torn down it has no display left to report the wake on.

Co-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Change-Id: I1318581758bada5498e6e713bd3bc58151174ef0
---
 services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp b/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
index 50115b3543..5bc30f4855 100644
--- a/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
+++ b/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
@@ -4373,6 +4373,13 @@ void SurfaceFlinger::setPowerModeInternal(const sp<DisplayDevice>& display, hal:
         mScheduler->setDisplayPowerState(mode == hal::PowerMode::ON);
     }
 
+    // The only event-driven signal the HDMI daemon has for its sleep timeout.
+    // The built-in screen's state, not the external's: a torn-down external
+    // has no display to report the wake on.
+    if (display->isPrimary()) {
+        property_set("sys.xdplus.screen_power", mode == hal::PowerMode::OFF ? "0" : "1");
+    }
+
     ALOGD("Finished setting power mode %d on display %s", mode, to_string(*displayId).c_str());
 }
 
